Relevant columns and their potential usage   

• "reference_bases": exact nucleotide string we want to filter on ('AT', 'TA').  
• "start_position": numeric coordinate – we will aggregate MIN / MAX.  
• "partition_date_please_ignore": the ingest date – use it to keep only 2015‑02‑20 rows.  
• "end_position": optional sanity‑check for ranges and extra exploration.  
• "alternate_bases": JSON array – not needed for the requested statistics but useful to inspect when troubleshooting.  
• "quality", "filter", "name"/"names", … : not required for the min / max task but handy for extended QA queries.  

Typical base condition that will appear in most queries  

WHERE "partition_date_please_ignore" = '2015-02-20'  

Optional extra condition for the task itself  

AND "reference_bases" IN ('AT','TA')  

Below are ten increasingly involved Snowflake SQL snippets (each limited to 20 rows where it makes sense) that help you explore the data step‑by‑step and finally produce the numbers you need.  Replace the LIMIT if you want to scan more lines.

